12:["$","section",null,{"className":"p-6 sm:p-8 bg-paper border border-rule border-l-[6px] border-l-flag-green rounded-card","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-sans font-bold text-base sm:text-lg text-ink mb-3","children":"Key constitutional takeaways"}],["$","ul",null,{"className":"space-y-2.5","children":[["$","li","0",{"className":"font-serif text-[17px] text-ink leading-relaxed flex items-start gap-2.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-sans font-black text-flag-green text-sm flex-shrink-0 mt-0.5","children":"•"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Article 33: Freedom of expression and the right to seek and share ideas."}]]}],["$","li","1",{"className":"font-serif text-[17px] text-ink leading-relaxed flex items-start gap-2.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-sans font-black text-flag-green text-sm flex-shrink-0 mt-0.5","children":"•"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Article 35: Right of access to information held by public and private entities."}]]}],["$","li","2",{"className":"font-serif text-[17px] text-ink leading-relaxed flex items-start gap-2.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-sans font-black text-flag-green text-sm flex-shrink-0 mt-0.5","children":"•"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Article 37: Right, peaceably and unarmed, to assemble, demonstrate, picket, and petition."}]]}],["$","li","3",{"className":"font-serif text-[17px] text-ink leading-relaxed flex items-start gap-2.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-sans font-black text-flag-green text-sm flex-shrink-0 mt-0.5","children":"•"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Article 43: Economic and social rights to health, housing, food, water, and education."}]]}]]}]]}]
13:["$","section",null,{"className":"space-y-6","children":[["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-[clamp(28px,3.6vw,44px)] font-black text-ink tracking-[-0.025em] leading-[1.05] font-sans","children":["Articles in Chapter ",4]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"font-serif text-ink-muted text-base mt-1","children":"Select an article to read its legal clauses and plain language meaning."}]]}],["$","$L21",null,{"articles":[{"articleNumber":19,"title":"Rights and fundamental freedoms","summary":"Establishes the Bill of Rights as the cornerstone of Kenya's democratic society, preserving human dignity and equality.","clauses":[{"clauseNumber":"(1)","text":"The Bill of Rights is an integral part of Kenya's democratic state and is the framework for social, economic and cultural policies."},{"clauseNumber":"(2)","text":"The purpose of recognising and protecting human rights and fundamental freedoms is to preserve the dignity of individuals and communities and to promote social justice and the realisation of the potential of all human beings."},{"clauseNumber":"(3)","text":"The rights and fundamental freedoms in the Bill of Rights: (a) belong to each individual and are not granted by the State; (b) do not exclude other rights and fundamental freedoms not mentioned in the Bill of Rights, in so far as they are consistent with the purposes of the Constitution; and (c) are subject only to the limitations contemplated in this Constitution."}],"plainExplanation":"Human rights are inherent to you as a person. The State does not give you rights, and therefore the State cannot arbitrarily withdraw them."},{"articleNumber":20,"title":"Application of Bill of Rights","summary":"Ensures the Bill of Rights applies to all law and binds all State organs and all persons.","clauses":[{"clauseNumber":"(1)","text":"The Bill of Rights applies to all law and binds all State organs and all persons."},{"clauseNumber":"(2)","text":"Every person shall enjoy the rights and fundamental freedoms in the Bill of Rights to the greatest extent consistent with the nature of the right or fundamental freedom."}],"plainExplanation":"Courts must interpret legal provisions in favor of expanding human freedoms rather than restricting them."},{"articleNumber":21,"title":"Implementation of rights and fundamental freedoms","summary":"Mandates that the State must observe, respect, protect, promote, and fulfill the rights in the Bill of Rights.","clauses":[{"clauseNumber":"(1)","text":"It is a fundamental duty of the State and every State organ to observe, respect, protect, promote and fulfil the rights and fundamental freedoms in the Bill of Rights."},{"clauseNumber":"(2)","text":"The State shall take legislative, policy and other measures, including the setting of standards, to achieve the progressive realisation of the rights guaranteed under Article 43."},{"clauseNumber":"(3)","text":"All State organs and all public officers have the duty to address the needs of vulnerable groups within society, including women, older members of society, persons with disabilities, children, youth, members of minority or marginalised communities, and members of particular ethnic, religious or cultural communities."}],"plainExplanation":"Public officials have a legal responsibility to provide basic services and protect vulnerable citizens from neglect or oppression."},{"articleNumber":22,"title":"Enforcement of Bill of Rights","summary":"Empowers any citizen to institute court proceedings claiming that a right or fundamental freedom has been denied, violated, or threatened.","clauses":[{"clauseNumber":"(1)","text":"Every person has the right to institute court proceedings claiming that a right or fundamental freedom in the Bill of Rights has been denied, violated or infringed, or is threatened."},{"clauseNumber":"(2)","text":"In addition to a person acting in their own interest, court proceedings under clause (1) may be instituted by: (a) a person acting on behalf of another person who cannot act in their own name; (b) a person acting as a member of, or in the interest of, a group or class of persons; (c) a person acting in the public interest; or (d) an association acting in the interest of one or more of its members."}],"plainExplanation":"You do not have to be the direct victim to sue the government. Any citizen or NGO can bring public interest litigation on behalf of others."},{"articleNumber":24,"title":"Limitation of rights and fundamental freedoms","summary":"Rights may be limited only by law, and only to the extent that the limitation is reasonable and justifiable in an open and democratic society.","clauses":[{"clauseNumber":"(1)","text":"A right or fundamental freedom in the Bill of Rights shall not be limited except by law, and then only to the extent that the limitation is reasonable and justifiable in an open and democratic society based on human dignity, equality and freedom."}],"plainExplanation":"Government cannot arbitrarily curtail your rights through administrative decrees or police pronouncements."},{"articleNumber":25,"title":"Fundamental rights that may not be limited","summary":"Lists absolute rights that cannot be suspended under any circumstances, even during a state of emergency.","clauses":[{"clauseNumber":"(1)","text":"Despite any other provision in this Constitution, the following rights and fundamental freedoms shall not be limited: (a) freedom from torture and cruel, inhuman or degrading treatment or punishment; (b) freedom from slavery or servitude; (c) the right to a fair trial; and (d) the right to an order of habeas corpus."}],"plainExplanation":"Torture, slavery, unfair trials, and holding detainees without judicial production are strictly illegal under all conditions."},{"articleNumber":26,"title":"Right to life","summary":"Every person has the right to life. Life begins at conception and may not be deprived arbitrarily.","clauses":[{"clauseNumber":"(1)","text":"Every person has the right to life."},{"clauseNumber":"(2)","text":"The life of a person begins at conception."},{"clauseNumber":"(3)","text":"A person shall not be deprived of life intentionally, except to the extent authorised by this Constitution or other written law."}],"plainExplanation":"Extrajudicial killings and unauthorized fatal police force are clear violations of the supreme law."},{"articleNumber":27,"title":"Equality and freedom from discrimination","summary":"Every person is equal before the law and has the right to equal protection and equal benefit of the law.","clauses":[{"clauseNumber":"(1)","text":"Every person is equal before the law and has the right to equal protection and equal benefit of the law."},{"clauseNumber":"(4)","text":"The State shall not discriminate directly or indirectly against any person on any ground, including race, sex, pregnancy, marital status, health status, ethnic or social origin, colour, age, disability, religion, conscience, belief, culture, dress, language or birth."}],"plainExplanation":"All Kenyans are entitled to equal treatment in public employment, civic opportunities, and justice administration."},{"articleNumber":28,"title":"Human dignity","summary":"Every person has inherent dignity and the right to have that dignity respected and protected.","clauses":[{"clauseNumber":"(1)","text":"Every person has inherent dignity and the right to have that dignity respected and protected."}],"plainExplanation":"State officials must treat suspects, detainees, and citizens with fundamental dignity at all times."},{"articleNumber":31,"title":"Privacy","summary":"Protects individuals from unlawful searches, seizure of possessions, and infringement of private communications.","clauses":[{"clauseNumber":"(1)","text":"Every person has the right to privacy, which includes the right not to have: (a) their person or home searched; (b) their property searched; (c) their possessions seized; (d) information relating to their family or private affairs unnecessarily required or revealed; or (e) the privacy of their communications infringed."}],"plainExplanation":"Police cannot search your person, home, or phone without lawful judicial authorization."},{"articleNumber":33,"title":"Freedom of expression","summary":"Guarantees the freedom to seek, receive, or impart information or ideas, freedom of artistic creativity, and academic freedom.","clauses":[{"clauseNumber":"(1)","text":"Every person has the right to freedom of expression, which includes: (a) freedom to seek, receive or impart information or ideas; (b) freedom of artistic creativity; and (c) academic freedom and freedom of scientific research."},{"clauseNumber":"(2)","text":"The right to freedom of expression does not extend to: (a) propaganda for war; (b) incitement to violence; (c) hate speech; or (d) advocacy of hatred that constitutes ethnic incitement, vilification of others or incitement to cause harm."}],"plainExplanation":"Citizens can freely critique government policies, produce investigative journalism, and express public opinion."},{"articleNumber":35,"title":"Access to information","summary":"Guarantees every citizen the right of access to information held by the State or another person required for rights protection.","clauses":[{"clauseNumber":"(1)","text":"Every citizen has the right of access to: (a) information held by the State; and (b) information held by another person and required for the exercise or protection of any right or fundamental freedom."},{"clauseNumber":"(2)","text":"Every person has the right to the correction or deletion of untrue or misleading information that affects the person."},{"clauseNumber":"(3)","text":"The State shall publish and publicise any important information affecting the nation."}],"plainExplanation":"Government procurement records, county budgets, public contracts, and official decisions must be made available upon request."},{"articleNumber":37,"title":"Assembly, demonstration, picketing and petition","summary":"Every person has the right, peaceably and unarmed, to assemble, to demonstrate, to picket, and to present petitions to public authorities.","clauses":[{"clauseNumber":"(1)","text":"Every person has the right, peaceably and unarmed, to assemble, to demonstrate, to picket, and to present petitions to public authorities."}],"plainExplanation":"Peaceful protest and demonstration are protected constitutional rights. Police require notice for security coordination, not permission."},{"articleNumber":43,"title":"Economic and social rights","summary":"Guarantees rights to health services, housing, sanitation, adequate food, clean water, social security, and education.","clauses":[{"clauseNumber":"(1)","text":"Every person has the right: (a) to the highest attainable standard of health, which includes the right to health care services, including reproductive health care; (b) to accessible and adequate housing, and to reasonable standards of sanitation; (c) to be free from hunger, and to have adequate food of acceptable quality; (d) to clean and safe water in adequate quantities; (e) to social security; and (f) to education."},{"clauseNumber":"(2)","text":"A person shall not be denied emergency medical treatment."}],"plainExplanation":"Socio-economic rights are justiciable legal entitlements that public budgetary decisions must respect and fulfill."},{"articleNumber":49,"title":"Rights of arrested persons","summary":"Requires immediate notification of the reason for arrest, access to legal counsel, and appearance before a court within 24 hours.","clauses":[{"clauseNumber":"(1)","text":"An arrested person has the right: (a) to be informed promptly, in language that the person understands, of the reason for the arrest; (b) to remain silent; (c) to communicate with an advocate, and other persons whose assistance is necessary; (d) not to be compelled to make any confession or admission; (f) to be brought before a court as soon as reasonably possible, but not later than twenty-four hours after being arrested; and (h) to be released on bond or bail, on reasonable conditions, pending a charge or trial, unless there are compelling reasons not to be released."}],"plainExplanation":"Holding suspects beyond 24 hours without court production is unconstitutional. Arrested individuals have an automatic right to apply for bail."},{"articleNumber":50,"title":"Fair hearing","summary":"Guarantees every accused person the right to a fair and public trial before an independent and impartial court.","clauses":[{"clauseNumber":"(1)","text":"Every person has the right to have any dispute that can be resolved by the application of law decided in a fair and public hearing before a court or, if appropriate, another independent and impartial tribunal or body."},{"clauseNumber":"(2)","text":"Every accused person has the right to a fair trial, which includes the right: (a) to be presumed innocent until the contrary is proved; (b) to be informed of the charge with sufficient detail to answer it; (c) to have adequate time and facilities to prepare a defence; (j) to be informed in advance of the evidence the prosecution intends to rely on, and to have reasonable access to that evidence; and (q) if convicted, to appeal to, or apply for review by, a higher court as prescribed by law."}],"plainExplanation":"Every accused person is innocent until proven guilty in an open court with full access to evidence."}],"chapterNumber":4}]]}]
